course: dictionary
lesson: 10518
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The word tyke is one of them. To pick the right meaning, read the words around it, which is the context. «In Tom and Jerry, Spike the bulldog guards his puppy Tyke» tells you Tyke is a dog, because it is a puppy that a bulldog guards.
explain: When adults say «You little tyke!» to a child, they mean a small, cheeky child, and they are smiling. The words around it show the feeling. A tyke can also be a person from Yorkshire, in the north of England, when it is written with a capital T.
explain: Always ask: which meaning fits this sentence? Then put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: «Tyke» can mean a small child, especially a cheeky or naughty one, said in a friendly way.
fact: «Tyke» with a capital T is a nickname for a person from Yorkshire, in the north of England.
fact: An old word, «tyke», can mean a dog of no special breed, which is a mongrel.
fact: A word with more than one meaning is a clue to read carefully: check the words around it.
exercise: «In Tom and Jerry, Spike the bulldog guards his puppy Tyke.» What is Tyke here? | *A dog of no special breed | A kind of cat | A small child who is cheeky | A person from Yorkshire
exercise: Dad smiles and says, «Don't be such a cheeky little tyke!» What does tyke mean? | A dog with a famous breed | *A small, naughty child, said in a friendly way | A person from Yorkshire | A bike with a flat tyre
exercise: Ella says, «I'm a Tyke, I was born in Yorkshire.» What does Tyke mean here? | A small, cheeky child | *A person from Yorkshire | A dog that barks at cats
test: «The cheeky tyke grinned at me.» What does tyke mean? | A bicycle | *A small, cheeky child | A dog of no special breed | A person from Yorkshire
test: You read «He is a little tyke» about your baby brother. What does the word suggest? | He is a dog with no owner | He is very old | He comes from the north of England | *He is a small child, and a bit naughty, said kindly
exam: You meet tyke in a sentence and you are not sure which meaning is right. What should you do first? | Look only at the capital letter and guess | *Read the words around it, then try each meaning in the sentence | Guess the meaning that sounds the most like a dog | Give up and skip the whole sentence
